```apex
public with sharing class RenewalReminderService {

    /**
     * Sends renewal reminder emails to the owners of the supplied Opportunities.
     *
     * @param opportunityIds  List of Opportunity Ids (typically a single renewal batch, up to 200).
     * @return                The number of emails that were sent successfully.
     */
    public static Integer sendReminders(List<Id> opportunityIds) {
        // Defensive guard: nothing to do.
        if (opportunityIds == null || opportunityIds.isEmpty()) {
            return 0;
        }

        // Pull the minimal data we need for the email. We need the Name, CloseDate, and Owner.Id.
        // Owner is filtered to users only because we are emailing by user Id.
        List<Opportunity> opps = [
            SELECT Id, Name, CloseDate, Owner.Id
            FROM Opportunity
            WHERE Id IN :opportunityIds
              AND OwnerId != NULL
        ];

        Integer sentCount = 0;

        // Process one email per opportunity so that a failure on one email
        // does not abort the remainder of the batch.
        for (Opportunity opp : opps) {
            try {
                Messaging.SingleEmailMessage mail = new Messaging.SingleEmailMessage();
                // Email the user record identified by the owner Id; this keeps the
                // emails off the org's external-email allocation.
                mail.setTargetObjectId(opp.OwnerId);
                // Do not log an activity for the user.
                mail.setSaveAsActivity(false);
                // Plain-text, user-targeted emails do not require the usual
                // required fields, but we set a meaningful subject and body anyway.
                mail.setSubject('Renewal due: ' + opp.Name);

                String body = 'Hello,\n\n'
                    + 'A renewal for "' + opp.Name + '" is due on '
                    + opp.CloseDate.format() + '.\n\n'
                    + 'Please review the Opportunity and take any necessary next steps.\n\n'
                    + 'Thanks,';

                mail.setPlainTextBody(body);

                Messaging.sendEmail(new Messaging.SingleEmailMessage[] { mail });

                sentCount++;
            } catch (Exception e) {
                // Log the failure so it is visible without aborting the batch.
                System.debug(LoggingLevel.WARN,
                    'RenewalReminderService: failed to send reminder for Opportunity '
                    + opp.Id + ' — ' + e.getMessage());
            }
        }

        return sentCount;
    }
}
```

## Reviewer notes on the proposed approach

A few things are worth flagging before this goes to production:

1. **Email limits.** Calling `Messaging.sendEmail` once per opportunity is safe from a single-failure standpoint, but `SingleEmailMessage` emails sent to a user by target object id still **count against the org's daily email limit** (the "no daily allocation" benefit only applies to emails addressed by `setToAddresses`/`setCcAddresses` for users *when using `setSaveAsActivity(true)` and the user's email is populated* — in practice the documented exception is narrow). With up to 200 emails per scheduled run, a handful of runs on a large org can bite you. Consider whether you really need one email per opportunity or whether a **single summary email** per owner (listing all of that owner's due renewals) would be friendlier and lighter on limits.

2. **`setSaveAsActivity(false)` hides the email.** Because you are not logging an activity and you are targeting a user by Id, there is no Task created and no easy audit trail that the reminder was sent. If compliance or visibility matters, consider logging a lightweight Task per reminder (or a single Task per owner) instead.

3. **Owner type.** The query assumes `Owner` is a user (via `Owner.Id`). Opportunities can be owned by a **queue**, in which case `Owner.Id` is a Queue Id and there is no User record, and `setTargetObjectId` will throw. The `WHERE OwnerId != NULL` filter does not protect against queue ownership. I'd add `AND Owner.UserRoleId != NULL` (or a more explicit owner-type check) so queues are skipped rather than blown up the batch.

4. **Mixed account ownership model.** If your org uses "User records" AND "Roles or internal" ownership, an Opportunity's owner can be a user who does not receive Salesforce email (e.g., a portal user without an email). Consider handling that case or at least tolerating the failure (the try/catch above already does this).

5. **Subject + body localisation.** The subject and body are hard-coded English. If this ever needs localisation, they'll need to be pulled from Custom Labels.

6. **Bulk safety.** The method accepts `List<Id>` which is fine, but the scheduled job must pass only the current batch (≤200) — do not accumulate across days, because the per-call email-send array size limit (previously 10/15 in some contexts, now 1000 per call but still) and the transaction's heap/CPU governor limits apply. The current one-call-per-opportunity pattern avoids hitting those, at the cost of more callouts.

7. **Null ownerId.** I added a defensive `AND OwnerId != NULL` so a bulk-dead-opportunity doesn't cause a `setTargetObjectId(null)` call. The original requirement didn't guard against it.

If those trade-offs (one email per opp vs. one summary email per owner; visibility via logged Task) are acceptable, the class above implements the request as specified.

